# Copy this file to .env (ignored by git) and change only what you need.

# Where the Vite dev server forwards /api calls: the backend's gateway. Only vite.config.ts
# reads it, so it never reaches the browser. Scheme, host and port, with no /api at the end:
# the app asks for /api and Vite passes it on.
#
# With `npm run dev` on your own machine use localhost. The Docker service name
# (http://gateway:8080) only resolves when Vite itself runs in a container on the backend's
# network; from your machine the name "gateway" does not resolve. The backend has to publish
# port 8080 on the host (the development stack does).
#
# A real environment variable of the same name wins over this file.
BACKEND_URL=http://localhost:8080

# Base URL for API calls in the browser.
#
# Leave it UNSET for local development: api.ts then falls back on a relative "/api", and the
# dev server forwards that to BACKEND_URL, which keeps everything on one origin and avoids CORS
# altogether.
#
# Set it for a production build only when the API is on another origin, which is the case on
# Railway: the app and the gateway have different domains. It has to END WITH /api, because
# every call appends its own path to it (https://api.example.com/api), and the gateway has to
# allow this app's origin in its FRONTEND_ORIGIN. The value is compiled into the app, so
# changing it needs a new build.
#
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com/api
